+## HID keyboard
+
+`usb: { controller: "uhci", keyboard: true }` adds a full-speed HID boot
+keyboard (QEMU's keyboard's IDs, `0627:0001`, "v86 USB Keyboard") on the
+port after the tablet's: class 3, subclass 1 (boot interface), protocol 1,
+one interrupt IN endpoint (8 bytes, every 10 ms), a report of eight
+modifiers, a reserved byte and six key usages (HID 1.11, appendix B.1).
+Reports go out when something changed, or after SET_IDLE's time; otherwise
+NAK. HID requests: GET/SET_IDLE, GET/SET_PROTOCOL, GET_REPORT, SET_REPORT
+(the LEDs), the HID and report descriptors.
+
+**The page's keys.** The bus's `keyboard-usage` message,
+`[usage, whether it went down]`, holds or releases one key: usages 0x04 to
+0x65 (HID's keyboard page: 0x04 "a", 0x28 Return, 0x29 Escape, 0x2C Space,
+0x3A to 0x45 the function keys, 0x4F to 0x52 the arrows…) and 0xE0 to 0xE7
+the modifiers (0xE0 left Control, 0xE1 left Shift, 0xE2 left Alt, 0xE3 left
+GUI — Command on a Mac). One transition per report: a down and its up never
+coalesce, so a keystroke is not lost when the guest polls slowly. A bus
+reset releases everything. The keyboard announces its use
+(`usb-keyboard-active`), as the tablet does.
+
+**Why.** macOS on a Mac has no PS/2 controller and no PS/2 driver; Lion
+10.7.5 ships `AppleHIDKeyboard` and `IOUSBFamily` but no `ApplePS2*`, so a
+PS/2 keyboard reaches nothing while this one binds. It is opt-in: a guest
+that has PS/2 keeps it.
+
